    "turn_idx": 113,
    "turn_text": "of the people who stormed the Capitol such that members of Congress had to be whisked away and barricaded and protected. Do you take any issue with my description of what happened that day? Well, I mean, look, what I've been saying from the very beginning of this is that Why was the capital not protected adequately? I've been around for a long time. Any type of little protest and you've got proper fencing put up. I think that remains a question. The capital has always been vulnerable to attacks like this, but I've been around for lots of protests. And you had a lot more security than what we had that day. I think that's a major question mark. Secondly, is there are laws on the books and if laws were broken, people need to be prosecuted. And what I've been saying from the beginning of this is I want to know every single person that forcefully, either broke windows, went through barricades. And I have yet to be able to see who those people are. In fact, those people, a lot of them haven't been prosecuted. And I think that's the question here is, is that people are saying that somehow it's there's some problem with people protesting the government. Now, that's never been the case. It's a fundamental right that people have to go to the US Capitol, the People's House, and protest the government. What they do not have a right to do is break windows and destroy federal property. And I want to see every single one of those people prosecuted, but it becomes this, you know, it's like, you know, all of a sudden it's like, oh, this insurrection. It's like, wait a second. No, it was a protest where people were able to break into the Capitol. That should have never happened.",
